<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Owen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1771">1771</a>–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Owen was a Welsh textile manufacturer and social reformer, and a founder of utopian socialism and the co-operative movement. From 1800 he managed the New Lanark <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, where in 1816 he opened an Institution for the Formation of Character offering free education. By 1817 he had formulated the goal of an eight-hour working day, with the slogan &quot;eight hours' labour, eight hours' recreation, eight hours' rest&quot;. His community at New Harmony, Indiana, lasted about two years, and the Grand National Consolidated Trades Union, which he briefly led, collapsed in 1834.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-owen.jpg" alt="Robert Owen" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Henry Brooke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Owen_by_William_Henry_Brooke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
